  "number": 6021,
  "title": "Describe Adaptive Card input errors instead of naming the input with them",
  "body": "> Fixes #5613\n\n## Changelog Entry\n\n### Fixed\n\n- Fixed [#5613](https://github.com/microsoft/BotFramework-WebChat/issues/5613), Adaptive Card input errors read as part of the input's name, in PR [#6021](https://github.com/microsoft/BotFramework-WebChat/pull/6021), by [@OEvgeny](https://github.com/OEvgeny)\n\n## Description\n\nAdaptive Cards puts the validation error's ID in the input's `aria-labelledby`, so screen readers read \"Role Please select the role Project Manager\" as its name; it never sets `aria-invalid` and keeps the ID after the error is gone. A new card mod moves the error to `aria-describedby`, sets `aria-invalid=\"true\"` while it is shown, and drops IDs that point to nothing. Not yet checked with Narrator or NVDA.\n\n## Design\n\n- The long-term fix is upstream, in `Input.getAllLabelIds()` (still in adaptivecards 3.0.6); the mod does nothing once the error is no longer in the name.\n- Not `aria-errormessage`: screen reader support is still uneven; `aria-describedby` is read everywhere.\n- Undo restores an attribute only if Adaptive Cards has not rewritten it (validation rewrites `aria-labelledby` in place).\n- Runs like the other mods: after render and on card DOM changes, one query per input.\n\n## Specific Changes\n\n- `useInputErrorShouldBeDescriptionModEffect.ts`: new mod, used by `AdaptiveCardRenderer.tsx`\n- `setOrRemoveAttributeIfFalseWithUndoIfUnchanged.ts`: compare-and-restore undo\n- `hack.inputErrorShouldBeDescriptionMod.*.html`, text, toggle, multi-select: snapshot 1 error (label is the name, error the description, invalid), 2 cleared (no dangling ID); `text` submits 2×\n- `….choiceSet.filtered.html`: snapshot 1, text box described by the error, invalid\n\n- [x] I have added tests and executed them locally\n- [ ] I have added `.changelog/<PR#>-<slug>.md`\n- [ ] I have updated documentation\n\n## Review Checklist\n\n> This section is for contributors to review your work.\n\n- [ ] Accessibility reviewed (tab order, content readability, alt text, color contrast)\n- [ ] Browser and platform compatibilities reviewed\n- [ ] CSS styles reviewed (minimal rules, no `z-index`)\n- [ ] Documents reviewed (docs, samples, live demo)\n- [ ] Internationalization reviewed (strings, unit formatting)\n- [ ] `package.json` and `package-lock.json` reviewed\n- [ ] Security reviewed (no data URIs, check for nonce leak)\n- [ ] Tests reviewed (coverage, legitimacy)\n",
